# mm.fs.local

The board's own storage, mounted where the program says: mount("/data")
gives a program files with no further setup, and what the files live on is
the platform's choice. On Linux it is a directory -- the device map's
directory.0, or the working directory when the map names none. On Pico it
will be littlefs on a region of program flash, once that provider exists; a
program reaching mm.fs.local is unavailable on a lane that binds no provider.

The program still decides where and when anything is mounted; only what is
mounted belongs to the platform. unmount detaches only volumes this module
attached, so a volume mounted at the same prefix by other means is never
handed to the provider.

The platform-interface marker makes the selected provider part of an
executable only when its closure reaches mm.fs.local; with none bound the
linkable fallback answers Unsupported.
